186530572 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f13d3f5c-4669-55f0-8d8e-50bb1b38e0c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530572 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186397210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530652 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530654 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530652 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530652 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1b/1b693165c8f6201e96f0a972746324c65a308d1f143f229c465b6aa22b7aeb16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}5\\left((x-y)\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=400,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(5,0),$ as shown above?\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530652 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06e31152-0f29-5cdd-9766-cb494216c61b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530652 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5\\left(x-y\\right)$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\left(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5\\left({\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =5\\left(-5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530659 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186133891.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530659 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "4fff02f7-01ae-5306-bce3-9da17a05246e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530671 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530690 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530671 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530671 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=2 x + y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 0.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530671 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3681ad8b-d269-5b68-8bae-a23d7eb16998" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530671 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(2)^2+(1)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 2 x (2 x + y))\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- 4 x^{2} - 2 x y)\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\sqrt{6} (- 4 x^{2} - 2 x y)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530672 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530678 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530672 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530672 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=2 x z^{2} + 2 y z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $-2\\le x\\le -1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530672 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37c4cfa9-0446-5895-b8a2-fde2c5de0dbf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530672 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=- 2 x + 2 y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=2 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 2 x + 2 y)^2+(2 x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 x (- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})^{2} + 2 y (- x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}))\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2 x^{5} - 8 x^{4} y + 16 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y + 8 x y^{4} + 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{(- 2 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1} (2 x^{5} - 8 x^{4} y + 16 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y + 8 x y^{4} + 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{3})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530674 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530698 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530674 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530674 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=- x z + y z^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530674 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c29642f-a472-5c91-80fe-7e7c9769bea9" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530674 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=- 4 x + 2 y,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})=2 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+(- 4 x + 2 y)^2+(2 x + 4 y)^2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(- x (- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2}) + y (- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2})^{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(4 x^{4} y - 8 x^{3} y^{2} + 2 x^{3} - 4 x^{2} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} y + 8 x y^{4} - 2 x y^{2} + 4 y^{5})\\cdot (\\sqrt{(- 4 x + 2 y)^{2} + (2 x + 4 y)^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\